  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how water vapour moves through two kinds of hygroscopic salt composite material separated by air gaps. On the left, both materials exchange vapour with the surrounding air, but the internal transport pathways differ strongly between a gel matrix and a non-gel matrix.\n\nIn the gel-based material, vapour appears to diffuse through a fine network of micrometre-scale pores. The enlarged view shows winding, irregular pore spaces with arrows spreading through them, suggesting relatively distributed transport. A further zoom highlights sorption at liquid–gas interfaces inside the porous gel, implying that uptake occurs around internal pore surfaces.\n\nIn contrast, the non-gel matrix shows vapour moving mainly through larger intercrystalline channels between solid regions. The magnified panel depicts more open, connected pathways that guide flow through the material. A final close-up indicates that after moving between crystals, vapour diffuses into a salt crystal and is sorbed or reacts within an internal “reaction area.”\n\nOverall, the image conveys two distinct moisture-transport mechanisms: pore-scale diffusion and interfacial sorption in the gel matrix versus channel-like intercrystalline transport followed by intracrystalline diffusion and reaction in the non-gel matrix.",
